{- | Gate for the interface-declaration permutation invariant over
   test/iface_order_fixtures. Replaces diff_compiler_iface_order.sh: the
   per-case loop, the ledger rot check, the build-witness tally and the final
   verdict.

   Runs under `medaka test --native`: it spawns `medaka check/run/build` and the
   built executables, and reads fixtures, neither of which the interpreter binds.

   A verification instrument, not a fix. Every ordering of a case's entry-module
   `interface` declarations is compiled and run, and reduced to a one-line
   signature. The signature must be invariant across orderings. A case whose
   signatures diverge is a live order dependence: a soundness bug, unless the
   case is ledgered in test/IFACE-ORDER-LEDGER.txt.

   The axis is new. test/diff_compiler_import_order.sh permutes import clauses,
   test/diff_compiler_dict_semantics_permute_test.mdk permutes impl blocks, and
   test/must_fail_fixtures/1182-two-ifaces-same-method-name-order-decides/ spends
   its control slot on impl-block order. None of them moves interface declarations.
   This gate moves only interface declarations, among their own slots; impls,
   imports and data declarations stay where they are.

   Why it was built: the identity re-keying of impl selection can be done two
   ways. A fix keyed on the interface NAME drains #1182's pin, because the
   impl-order dependence goes away, while moving the dependence onto the
   interface-declaration order. The existing pins cannot tell those outcomes
   apart. This gate can; see the four-state table below.

   ## The signature

     check=<exit>;codes=<sorted,comma>;schemes=<check stdout>;run=<exit>:<stdout>;build=<exit>/<exec-exit>:<stdout>

   check     The exit code of `medaka check --json`: the verdict cell. On this
             axis accept versus reject is the strongest reading a permutation
             can give.
   codes     Every "code" in the `check --json` output, sorted as a multiset.
             Diagnostics come out in source order and permutation moves source
             lines, so the sort is what keeps the cell invariant. Read it by code
             prefix, not by emptiness (see the four-state table).
   schemes   The plain `medaka check` scheme dump, selected by format (a
             `<name> : <type>` line at column 0), not by stream. Diagnostics are not
             reliably on one stream: resolve-stage ones land on stderr and
             type-stage ones on stdout, so a whole-stdout capture would embed the
             harness's temp path. A binding inferred at a different type while its
             printed value coincides shows up here.
   run       `medaka run` exit code and stdout (the interpreter).
   build     `medaka build` exit code, then the built binary's exit code and stdout
             ("-" if no binary was produced). This is not redundant with run: a
             case can build, segfault the binary at exit 139, and still have the
             interpreter panic. The exec-exit cell is the only place that is
             recorded.

   Output is escaped so a signature is always one line: backslash becomes two
   backslashes, `|` becomes `\p`, `;` becomes `\s`, tab becomes `\t` and newline
   becomes `\n`. `sigFor` builds the signature and `escapeOut` escapes its parts.

   Deliberately not compared: diagnostic message text (it may enumerate
   declarations in source order on purpose); diagnostic ranges (moving
   declarations of unequal line count shifts every line below them, so a location
   diff is an artifact); stderr of run and build (panic text is not uniformly
   location-free, and build's log embeds the source path); the order of
   diagnostics; and the emitted IR (the selected impl is observed through
   behaviour).

   ## Orderings and scope

   Every ordering is tried when there are at most four interface declarations.
   Above that the set is identity, reversal and every adjacent transposition. The
   shell gate printed PARTIAL on the row in that case; this test prints no such
   marker, so a reader who needs to know whether a case was graded on the full set
   must count its interface declarations.

   Scope limits. Only the entry module's interface declarations are permuted. An
   order dependence decided by an imported module's interface order is not covered,
   and nothing else covers it either. Interfaces move only among their own slots,
   so none crosses an impl or a data declaration. A dependence that needs such a
   crossing is out of scope by construction: that move can change whether the
   program parses at all, which is not the cosmetic reordering this gate is about.
   Every case is single-file today. The case-directory-plus-case.txt unit is kept
   anyway: it is what the ledger's issue-number naming lock hangs off, and it
   leaves room for a multi-module case later.

   ## The four-state table

   The gate's deliverable is that each future reads differently. A probe that
   said only same or different would collapse states 2 and 3, which is the
   confusion the identity fix is at risk of.

   State 1, today: order-dependent on both axes. #1182's must-fail pin reproduces
   and is green. A ledgered row reports KNOWN-BAD, still diverging, as pinned.

   State 2, a name-scoped fix (the trap): #1182's impl-order pin drains, and a
   reader who has only that pin concludes #1182 is fixed. This gate: a ledgered
   row still diverges. If the signatures are unchanged the row stays KNOWN-BAD;
   if the divergence moved at all the row goes red with "the divergence MOVED". This
   is the one state in which this gate does not go red by itself. Its backstop is
   test/must_fail_census.sh HALF 1, which is directory-driven: it fires on a closed
   issue whose must-fail fixture carries an issue line, and it never reads this
   gate's verdict. Do not remove that coupling. The rows that named #1182 and #1620
   were removed after Q1 landed (see test/IFACE-ORDER-LEDGER.txt), so that census
   half is now the only backstop for those two issues.

   State 3, Q1 (resolve rejects the ambiguous occurrence): the case converges to one
   signature, with check=1 and an R-* code in every ordering. This gate: a ledgered
   row goes red as DRAINED and prints the converged signature.

   State 4, identity-keyed selection with no resolve rejection: also converges to one
   signature and also goes red as DRAINED, but the code is not R-*. Two shapes count:
   check=0 with codes empty and a stable run or build value, where the selected impl
   typechecks and the program runs; or check=1 with a T-* code, where selection was
   deterministic and the impl it chose then failed typecheck. The worked example is
   an obligation case that declares only one impl: a fix that binds the method to
   the other interface converges on check=1;codes=T-NO-IMPL in every ordering. That
   is a type error, not an ambiguity rejection, and must not be read as state 3.

   The discriminator is the code prefix: R-* means state 3, T-* or empty means
   state 4. Neither the exit code nor "codes is non-empty" tells them apart.

   ## What the table cannot tell you

   Which kind of keying landed. Two single-file cases with distinctly named
   interfaces behave identically under a name-keyed fix and an identity-keyed one,
   so a drain here is not evidence that the key is module-qualified. Two interfaces
   of the same name in one file are rejected by resolve (`Duplicate interface`), so
   that shape cannot be written in this corpus. Two of the same name in different
   modules are legal, but this gate permutes only the entry module, so the entry
   holds one of them and the permuter stops with "need >=2 top-level interface
   declarations, found 1". Answering the keying question needs a cross-module probe;
   #1258 is that shape.

   ## Messages

   DRAINED: the case converged to one signature while a ledger row pins a
   divergence. Read the converged signature against the four states before closing
   anything. Then close the issue, delete the row and keep the fixture; without a
   row it is graded as invariant and becomes the regression guard.

   KNOWN-BAD, the divergence MOVED: the observed signatures differ from the pinned
   ones. Re-derive the right answer before re-pinning. Still divergent is not fixed.

   "N ordering(s) produced M DISTINCT signatures": an unledgered case diverges.
   Either fix it, or, if it is a known open bug, add a ledger row. A control must
   never be ledgered; if one diverges, the finding is larger than any row claims,
   so file it.

   The rest are structural. A ledger row must name an open issue and a directory
   named `<issue>-...`, must pin at least two signatures, and must name a case
   directory that exists. A case directory must hold case.txt. Zero orderings or
   zero cases is a failure, never a pass. The build arm must produce at least one
   executable somewhere in the corpus, or it graded nothing.

   ## The ledger

   test/IFACE-ORDER-LEDGER.txt pins, per case, the distinct signatures the case must
   keep producing. A row asserts a divergence, which is a live soundness bug shipping
   on main, and names the open issue that owns it. The gate stays green while rows
   exist, because a gate that lands red teaches people to ignore it; the rows are
   what keep the green honest. Each row drains itself: when its divergence collapses
   the row goes red. Whether an issue is open cannot be checked offline; a reviewer
   checks it with `gh issue view <n> --json state`. -}

import test_process.{medakaBin, underRoot, boundedVerbSeconds, withScratchDir}
import test.{
  Expectation, expectAll, expectEach, expectAtLeast, expectSatisfies, fail, pass
}
import fs.{fixtureFiles, fixtureDirs, isDir, isFile, mkdirAll}
import regex.{Regex, isMatch, mustCompile}
import string as S
import list.{head, sort, take, drop, reverse, removeAt}

corpus : <IO> String
corpus = underRoot "test/iface_order_fixtures"

ledgerPath : <IO> String
ledgerPath = underRoot "test/IFACE-ORDER-LEDGER.txt"

ifaceRe : Regex
ifaceRe = mustCompile "^(public[ \t]+)?(export[ \t]+)?interface[ \t]"

schemeRe : Regex
schemeRe = mustCompile "^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_']* : "

issueRe : Regex
issueRe = mustCompile "^#[0-9]"

-- ── small helpers ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

nthOf : Int -> List a -> a
nthOf i xs = match head (drop i xs)
  Some x => x
  None => panic "nthOf: index out of range"

lastOf : List a -> a
lastOf xs = match head (reverse xs)
  Some x => x
  None => panic "lastOf: empty list"

firstOr : a -> List a -> a
firstOr d xs = match head xs
  Some x => x
  None => d

upto : Int -> List Int
upto n = if n <= 0 then [] else upto (n - 1) ++ [n - 1]

-- Sorted, duplicates removed: the script's `sort -u`.
sortUnique : List String -> List String
sortUnique xs =
  fold (acc x => if any (== x) acc then acc else acc ++ [x]) [] (sort xs)

-- The text of `key` on the first line that starts with it, trimmed; "" if none.
fieldOf : String -> String -> String
fieldOf key text = match filter (l => S.startsWith key l) (S.lines text)
  [] => ""
  l :: _ => S.trim (S.drop (S.length key) l)

indentJoin : List String -> String
indentJoin xs = S.join "\n       " xs

-- ── the ledger (test/IFACE-ORDER-LEDGER.txt) ──────────────────────────────
-- A row is `case | issue | sig | sig ...`. The awk reader's rules: comments and
-- blank lines are skipped, a row needs at least three `|` fields, and the pinned
-- signatures are fields 3.. trimmed, non-empty, sorted and unique.

data LedgerRow = LedgerRow String String (List String)

isBlankOrComment : String -> Bool
isBlankOrComment line =
  let t = S.trim line
  t == "" || S.startsWith "#" t

parseRow : String -> Option LedgerRow
parseRow line =
  if isBlankOrComment line || length (S.split "|" line) < 3 then
    None
  else
    parseFields (S.split "|" line)

parseFields : List String -> Option LedgerRow
parseFields parts =
  let pinned = sortUnique (filter (/= "") (map S.trim (drop 2 parts)))
  Some (LedgerRow (S.trim (nthOf 0 parts)) (S.trim (nthOf 1 parts)) pinned)

keepSome : List (Option a) -> List a
keepSome [] = []
keepSome (None :: rest) = keepSome rest
keepSome ((Some x) :: rest) = x :: keepSome rest

ledgerRowsOf : String -> List LedgerRow
ledgerRowsOf text = keepSome (map parseRow (S.lines text))

rowCase : LedgerRow -> String
rowCase (LedgerRow c _ _) = c

-- The first row naming this case, as the script's `head -1` picks it.
rowFor : String -> List LedgerRow -> Option LedgerRow
rowFor _ [] = None
rowFor name (r :: rest) = if rowCase r == name then Some r else rowFor name rest

-- ── the permuter ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- Operates on TOP-LEVEL CHUNKS, as the script's embedded perl does: a chunk starts
-- at a line whose first character is not whitespace and runs to the next such
-- line, so an indented body or a blank line attaches to the chunk above it.
-- Interface chunks are permuted across their own slots; every other chunk stays.

isWs : String -> Bool
isWs c = c == " " || c == "\t" || c == "\r"

isTop : String -> Bool
isTop l = l /= "" && not (isWs (S.take 1 l))

isIfaceChunk : List String -> Bool
isIfaceChunk [] = False
isIfaceChunk (h :: _) = isMatch ifaceRe h

prependLine : String -> (List String, List String) -> (List String, List String)
prependLine l (body, after) = (l :: body, after)

-- Lines up to the next top-level line, and the rest.
spanBody : List String -> (List String, List String)
spanBody [] = ([], [])
spanBody (l :: rest) =
  if isTop l then ([], l :: rest) else prependLine l (spanBody rest)

attachBody : String -> (List String, List String) -> List (List String)
attachBody h (body, after) = (h :: body) :: groupChunks after

groupChunks : List String -> List (List String)
groupChunks [] = []
groupChunks (h :: rest) = attachBody h (spanBody rest)

-- Replace the interface chunks, in order, with `repl`; every other chunk stays.
rebuild : List (List String) -> List (List String) -> List (List String)
rebuild [] _ = []
rebuild (c :: cs) repl =
  if isIfaceChunk c then
    nthOf 0 repl :: rebuild cs (drop 1 repl)
  else
    c :: rebuild cs repl

-- Ordering `o` puts original interface chunk o[k] in slot k.
permute : List (List String) -> List Int -> List (List String)
permute chunks o =
  rebuild chunks (map (i => nthOf i (filter isIfaceChunk chunks)) o)

renderChunks : List (List String) -> String
renderChunks chunks = S.concat (map (c => S.concat (map (++ "\n") c)) chunks)

flatLists : List (List a) -> List a
flatLists xss = fold (++) [] xss

-- All permutations of xs, in the script's perl order.
permsOf : List Int -> List (List Int)
permsOf xs =
  if length xs == 0 then
    [[]]
  else
    flatLists
      (map
        (i => map (nthOf i xs :: _) (permsOf (removeAt i xs)))
        (upto (length xs)))

swapAt : Int -> List Int -> List Int
swapAt i xs = take i xs ++ [nthOf (i + 1) xs, nthOf i xs] ++ drop (i + 2) xs

-- Every permutation when n <= 4; otherwise identity, reversal and every adjacent
-- transposition (the script's PARTIAL set, which it marks on the row).
orderSet : Int -> List (List Int)
orderSet n =
  if n <= 4 then
    permsOf (upto n)
  else
    [upto n, reverse (upto n)] ++ map (i => swapAt i (upto n)) (upto (n - 1))

-- ── signature ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- Escaping of the observed output, applied in the script's esc() order. This
-- encodes the signature's own separators (`\`, `|`, `;`, tab, newline), which
-- is not `regex.escape` (regex metacharacters); the lint rule matches on the
-- `String -> String` signature alone.
escapeOut : String -> String
-- lint-disable-next-line rule-stdlib-reimpl
escapeOut s =
  S.replaceAll
    "\n"
    "\\n"
    (S.replaceAll
      "\t"
      "\\t"
      (S.replaceAll
        ";"
        "\\s"
        (S.replaceAll "|" "\\p" (S.replaceAll "\\" "\\\\" s))))

-- Every "code" in `check --json` output, sorted, comma-joined.
codesOf : String -> String
codesOf text =
  S.join
    ","
    (sort
      (map
        (p => firstOr "" (S.split "\"" p))
        (drop 1 (S.split "\"code\":\"" text))))

-- Scheme lines (`<name> : <type>` at column 0), selected by format, not stream.
schemesOf : String -> String
schemesOf text =
  S.concat (map (++ "\n") (filter (l => isMatch schemeRe l) (S.lines text)))

data OrdSig = OrdSig String String Bool

sigOf : OrdSig -> String
sigOf (OrdSig _ s _) = s

builtOf : OrdSig -> Bool
builtOf (OrdSig _ _ b) = b

execOf : String -> <Exec> Result String (String, String)
execOf bin = match boundedVerbSeconds 120 bin []
  Err e => Err "could not run the built binary: \{e}"
  Ok (x, xo, _) => Ok (intToString x, xo)

-- One signature for the case whose files are in `work`, as the script's sig_for() formats it.
sigFor : String ->
  String ->
  <Exec, IO, FileRead, FileWrite> Result String (String, Bool)
sigFor work entry = do
  let file = "\{work}/\{entry}"
  let bin = "\{work}/.bin"
  (checkCode, co, ce) <- boundedVerbSeconds 120 medakaBin [
    "check", "--json", file
  ]
  (_, plain, _) <- boundedVerbSeconds 120 medakaBin ["check", file]
  (runCode, ro, _) <- boundedVerbSeconds 120 medakaBin ["run", file]
  (buildCode, _, _) <- boundedVerbSeconds 120 medakaBin [
    "build", file, "-o", bin
  ]
  (execCode, xo) <- if buildCode == 0 then execOf bin else Ok ("-", "")
  let sig =
    "check=\{intToString checkCode};codes=\{codesOf (co ++ ce)};schemes=\{escapeOut (schemesOf plain)};run=\{intToString runCode}:\{escapeOut ro};build=\{intToString buildCode}/\{execCode}:\{escapeOut xo}"
  Ok (sig, buildCode == 0)

-- ── one case ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

copyAll : String -> List String -> <IO, FileRead, FileWrite> Result String Bool
copyAll _ [] = Ok True
copyAll work (f :: rest) = do
  text <- readFile f
  _ <- writeFile "\{work}/\{lastOf (S.split "/" f)}" text
  copyAll work rest

-- Each ordering gets a fresh work directory: the case's files, then the permuted entry.
runOrders : String ->
  String ->
  String ->
  List (List String) ->
  List (List Int) ->
  Int ->
  <Exec, IO, FileRead, FileWrite> Result String (List OrdSig)
runOrders _ _ _ _ [] _ = Ok []
runOrders cdir base entry chunks (o :: rest) k = do
  let work = "\{base}/p\{intToString k}"
  _ <- mkdirAll work
  files <- fixtureFiles cdir
  _ <- copyAll work files
  _ <- writeFile "\{work}/\{entry}" (renderChunks (permute chunks o))
  (sig, built) <- sigFor work entry
  more <- runOrders cdir base entry chunks rest (k + 1)
  Ok (OrdSig (S.join "," (map intToString o)) sig built :: more)

-- The verdict for a case with at least one ordering. Returns the built count too.
judge : List LedgerRow -> String -> String -> List OrdSig -> (Int, Expectation)
judge rows name what xs =
  if length xs == 0 then
    (0, fail "\{name}: produced ZERO orderings — the permuter ran nothing")
  else
    judgeOrdered
      rows
      name
      what
      xs
      (length (filter builtOf xs))
      (sortUnique (map sigOf xs))

judgeOrdered : List LedgerRow ->
  String ->
  String ->
  List OrdSig ->
  Int ->
  List String ->
  (Int, Expectation)
judgeOrdered rows name what xs built distinct = match rowFor name rows
  None => (built, unledgered name what (length xs) distinct)
  Some (LedgerRow _ issue pinned) =>
    (built, ledgered name issue pinned distinct)

unledgered : String -> String -> Int -> List String -> Expectation
unledgered name what nord distinct =
  if length distinct == 1 then
    pass
  else
    fail
      "\{name}: \{intToString nord} ordering(s) produced \{intToString (length distinct)} DISTINCT signatures — the answer depends on interface-declaration order\n       \{indentJoin distinct}\n  \{name}: \{what}\n     Either FIX it, or, if it is a known OPEN bug, add a row to test/IFACE-ORDER-LEDGER.txt.\n     A row is a statement that a soundness bug is shipping, not a way to make this green.\n     If this case is a CONTROL, do NOT ledger it."

ledgered : String -> String -> List String -> List String -> Expectation
ledgered name issue pinned distinct =
  if not (isMatch issueRe issue) then
    fail
      "\{name}: ledger row's issue field is \"\{issue}\" — it must be #<number> of an OPEN issue"
  else
    ledgeredNamed name issue pinned distinct

ledgeredNamed : String -> String -> List String -> List String -> Expectation
ledgeredNamed name issue pinned distinct =
  if not (S.startsWith (S.drop 1 issue ++ "-") name) then
    fail
      "\{name}: ledgered against \{issue} but the directory is not named \"\{S.drop 1 issue}-...\""
  else if length pinned < 2 then
    fail
      "\{name}: ledger row pins \{intToString (length pinned)} signature(s); a row asserts a DIVERGENCE, so it needs >=2"
  else if length distinct == 1 then
    fail
      "\{name}: DRAINED — \{issue} no longer diverges under interface-declaration permutation\n       observed signature: \{indentJoin distinct}\n     READ THE CONVERGED SIGNATURE: check=1 with an R-* code means the ambiguity is now\n     REJECTED at resolve (state 3); check=0 with codes= empty, or check=1 with a T-* code,\n     means selection is now deterministic (state 4). Convergence does not mean fully fixed:\n     check the sibling axes, then close the issue, delete the row, and keep the fixture."
  else if distinct == pinned then
    pass
  else
    fail
      "\{name}: KNOWN-BAD \{issue}: the divergence MOVED. pinned and observed signatures differ.\n       pinned:   \{indentJoin pinned}\n       observed: \{indentJoin distinct}\n     Re-derive the right answer before re-pinning. STILL DIVERGENT IS NOT FIXED."

-- Runs every ordering of one case under `base`, the test's own scratch tree.
caseOrders : List LedgerRow ->
  String ->
  String ->
  String ->
  String ->
  String ->
  List (List String) ->
  List (List Int) ->
  <Exec, IO, FileRead, FileWrite> (Int, Expectation)
caseOrders rows base name cdir entry what chunks ords =
  match runOrders cdir "\{base}/\{name}" entry chunks ords 1
    Err e => (0, fail "\{name}: PERMUTER could not write an ordering: \{e}")
    Ok xs => judge rows name what xs

caseChunks : List LedgerRow ->
  String ->
  String ->
  String ->
  String ->
  String ->
  List (List String) ->
  <Exec, IO, FileRead, FileWrite> (Int, Expectation)
caseChunks rows base name cdir entry what chunks =
  if length (filter isIfaceChunk chunks) < 2 then
    (
      0,
      fail
        "\{name}: PERMUTER: need >=2 top-level interface declarations, found \{intToString (length (filter isIfaceChunk chunks))}",
    )
  else
    caseOrders
      rows
      base
      name
      cdir
      entry
      what
      chunks
      (orderSet (length (filter isIfaceChunk chunks)))

caseEntry : List LedgerRow ->
  String ->
  String ->
  String ->
  String ->
  String ->
  <Exec, IO, FileRead, FileWrite> (Int, Expectation)
caseEntry rows base name cdir entry what = match readFile "\{cdir}/\{entry}"
  Err _ => (
    0,
    fail "\{name}: case.txt names entry \"\{entry}\", which is not a file here",
  )
  Ok text =>
    caseChunks rows base name cdir entry what (groupChunks (S.lines text))

caseSpec : List LedgerRow ->
  String ->
  String ->
  String ->
  <Exec, IO, FileRead, FileWrite> (Int, Expectation)
caseSpec rows base name cdir = match readFile (cdir ++ "/case.txt")
  Err _ =>
    (0, fail "\{name}: no case.txt (a case directory must declare its entry)")
  Ok spec =>
    caseEntry rows base name cdir (fieldOf "entry:" spec) (fieldOf "what:" spec)

-- (built count, verdict) for one case directory name under the corpus.
caseRun : List LedgerRow ->
  String ->
  String ->
  <Exec, IO, FileRead, FileWrite> (Int, Expectation)
caseRun rows base name = caseSpec rows base name "\{corpus}/\{name}"

-- Case directories, derived from the fixture file listing: the first path segment
-- under the corpus root, for every file that sits at least one directory down.
inCase : String -> String -> Bool
inCase prefix f =
  S.startsWith prefix f && length (S.split "/" (S.drop (S.length prefix) f)) > 1

caseOf : String -> String -> String
caseOf prefix f = firstOr "" (S.split "/" (S.drop (S.length prefix) f))

caseNames : String -> List String -> List String
caseNames prefix files =
  sortUnique (map (f => caseOf prefix f) (filter (f => inCase prefix f) files))

resOf : (String, (Int, Expectation)) -> (String, Expectation)
resOf (nm, (_, e)) = (nm, e)

-- The orderings of this case whose `build` exited 0.
buildsOfRes : (String, (Int, Expectation)) -> Int
buildsOfRes (_, (b, _)) = b

-- A ledger row must name a case directory that exists. The raw line is tested for
-- blank or `#` (no trim first), as the script's `case` did.
isRawSkip : String -> Bool
isRawSkip line = line == "" || S.startsWith "#" line

rawCaseOf : String -> String
rawCaseOf line = S.trim (firstOr "" (S.split "|" line))

rotCase : String -> <IO, FileRead> Expectation
rotCase c = match isDir "\{corpus}/\{c}"
  Err e => fail "could not stat ledger case \"\{c}\": \{e}"
  Ok True => pass
  Ok False =>
    fail
      "ledger names case \"\{c}\", which does not exist under test/iface_order_fixtures/"

rotCheck : String -> <IO, FileRead> Expectation
rotCheck ledgerText =
  expectAll
    (map
      rotCase
      (filter
        (/= "")
        (map rawCaseOf (filter (l => not (isRawSkip l)) (S.lines ledgerText)))))

-- Every subdirectory of the corpus is a case, so each must declare one. fixtureFiles
-- cannot see an empty directory; fixtureDirs lists it.
specCheck : String -> <IO, FileRead> Expectation
specCheck dir = match isFile "\{dir}/case.txt"
  Err e => fail "could not stat \{dir}/case.txt: \{e}"
  Ok True => pass
  Ok False =>
    fail "\{dir}: a case directory has no case.txt (it must declare its entry)"

zeroCheck : List String -> Expectation
zeroCheck names =
  if length names == 0 then
    fail
      "graded ZERO cases — the corpus derivation found nothing. This is not a pass."
  else
    pass

-- The BUILD arm must have produced at least one executable somewhere in the corpus.
buildWitness : Int -> Expectation
buildWitness builds =
  if builds == 0 then
    fail
      "the BUILD arm produced no executable anywhere in the corpus — it graded nothing, and an arm that graded nothing must not report green."
  else
    pass

rowLine : LedgerRow -> String
rowLine (LedgerRow c issue _) = "  \{c}  \{issue}"

rowLines : List LedgerRow -> String
rowLines rows = S.join "\n" (map rowLine rows)

-- A ledgered row is a soundness bug shipping on main. The banner is the pass label
-- of an always-true check, so it is named on every run.
ledgerBanner : List LedgerRow -> Expectation
ledgerBanner rows =
  if length rows == 0 then
    pass
  else
    expectSatisfies
      "LEDGERED — interface-declaration-order bugs shipping on main right now:\n\{rowLines rows}\n  (each drains itself: when the divergence collapses, its row goes RED here)"
      (b => b)
      True

iterBody : String -> <Exec, IO, FileRead, FileWrite> Expectation
iterBody base = match readFile ledgerPath
  Err e => fail "could not read the ledger: \{e}"
  Ok ledgerText => match fixtureFiles corpus
    Err e => fail "could not list the corpus: \{e}"
    Ok files => match fixtureDirs corpus
      Err e => fail "could not list the corpus directories: \{e}"
      Ok dirs =>
        let rows = ledgerRowsOf ledgerText
        let names = caseNames (corpus ++ "/") files
        let results = map (nm => (nm, caseRun rows base nm)) names
        let builds = fold (acc r => acc + buildsOfRes r) 0 results
        expectAll [
          expectAtLeast 5 (length names),
          zeroCheck names,
          expectEach (map resOf results),
          rotCheck ledgerText,
          expectAll (map specCheck dirs),
          buildWitness builds,
          ledgerBanner rows,
        ]

test "iface_order: every case is invariant or ledgered-and-still-diverging" =
  withScratchDir iterBody
